from pathlib import Path
R=Path(__file__).resolve().parent/'repo';p=R/'PRODUCT.md';s=p.read_text()
def sub(a,b):
 global s
 assert a in s,a[:90]
 s=s.replace(a,b)
start=s.index('Enclave (enclave.host) is a verifiable compute platform')
end=s.index('\n## Positioning',start)
s=s[:start]+'''Enclave (enclave.host) is a commercial, source-available compute platform under active development. Its goal is one portable app bundle and one versioned domain contract across supported hosts, with protections that a customer can verify for the deployment they choose. Hardware isolation, host-OS exclusion and protection from the machine's operator are separate claims: a running VM or an attestation report does not establish all three. The target architecture includes a common isolation contract, confidential-hardware backends and Enclave Shield's CPU isolation and masked GPU offload components. Availability and demonstrated guarantees must be stated separately for each component and backend.

The implementation notes below retain the 2026-10-14 deployment snapshot and subsequent evidence explicitly dated in the linked records. They are not a fresh inventory of online hosts or a certification of the full architecture. Current capacity comes from the live fleet; security claims require the relevant verified evidence and its limits. Success means deployments funded and running by customers who can understand and verify the protection they receive.

## Public Claims Policy

**Updated 2026-10-21.** Earlier positioning guidance was written while Enclave was under development and treated the intended architecture as the product narrative. It is superseded by this policy. Historical design notes describe their own stage of development; they neither establish current availability nor show that a feature was deployed at the time.

- **Separate availability from intended design.** Use present tense for implemented behavior only within its demonstrated deployment scope. Describe unfinished work as in development, planned or research, as appropriate. A component working on one named host is not evidence of fleet-wide support.
- **Put material limits beside the claim.** Readers must be able to distinguish current capabilities, experimental implementations and future plans in the overview, feature description and purchase flow itself. A distant engineering note or link cannot qualify an otherwise unconditional privacy promise. Status badges are optional; the information is required.
- **State the threat model.** Identify which parties remain trusted and what data or metadata they can access. Distinguish per-app separation, host-OS exclusion, operator protection and GPU offload. Incomplete evidence, such as Windows host exclusion, must not become a guarantee through branding or platform documentation alone.
- **Tie claims to evidence.** For security or deployment claims, record the component, backend, tested configuration, evidence date and scope, plus known gaps. Code present in the repository, successful builds, admission rules and laboratory results are not by themselves proof of production deployment or independent security validation. Say when review is internal and when no external audit exists.
- **Keep surfaces consistent.** Apply these rules to the homepage, README, catalog, deployment flow, product documentation and agent-generated copy. When evidence changes, update the claim and its nearby qualification together. If current deployment or evidence cannot be checked, retain the dated scope or say it is unverified; do not silently promote it to current availability.

''' +s[end:]
start=s.index('Lead claim (user-confirmed):')
end=s.index('\nFour pillars,',start)
s=s[:start]+'''Lead outcome: **compute that cannot see your data**. This is a positioning line, not a blanket guarantee across every host, component or data path. Any use must immediately explain which party is excluded for the selected deployment and which parties remain trusted. A claim that the ordinary host OS cannot read an app's memory requires evidence for that backend; protection from the operator requires the additional confidential-hardware guarantee and its stated assumptions. The Windows Shield deployment recorded in the 2026-10-14 snapshot has per-app partitions, but our host-memory test (E3, docs/security/nucbox-custom-vm-verifier.md V8) is recorded as not run and records say `host_excluded: no`. Describe the demonstrated separation and the remaining trusted parties; do not advertise host exclusion as proven.''' +s[end:]
sub('The public marketing copy describes the full target architecture as the product, in direct present tense (what Enclave does, how hosts, apps and GPU isolation work, what customers receive), with no roadmap, status or readiness framing ("we are building", "being built", "planned", "experimental", "future", "eventually" never appear in market copy). Empirical proof and current deployment facts stay exact in clearly technical contexts: the dated "Engineering evidence" note at the end of Develop > Architecture, the live fleet panel, and docs/site-claims-2026-09-23.md.', 'Present these pillars as the architectural direction, and label each implementation by the availability and evidence actually established for it. Public copy carries the relevant limits next to each claim. The dated "Engineering evidence" note, live fleet panel and docs/site-claims-2026-09-23.md provide supporting detail; the last is a historical claims record, not a current readiness assessment.')
sub('Supporting mechanisms (never lead, always available as proof): the full chain of trust is verifiable client-side in the browser before a byte is sent', 'Supporting mechanisms (explain their scope and gaps): supported verification paths check evidence client-side; do not imply that every deployment and data path is verified before any data is sent. The documented confidential-server check covers')
sub('No readiness taxonomy on the public site: the words available / experimental / planned, status badges, and status boards are not how the site is organized (removed 2026-09-23 at the owner\'s direction). One dated engineering note (Develop > Architecture > "Engineering evidence") records what has been demonstrated, what has not, and what is attached; that note, the fleet panel and repository documentation hold milestone evidence (M1 to M4b, the consumer machine\'s gaps, the Shield engine\'s deployment) and never define the narrative or the level names.', 'Use plain readiness language wherever it changes a customer\'s decision: available on specified hosts, experimental, in development, or planned. No particular badge design or site-wide status board is required. The dated engineering note (Develop > Architecture > "Engineering evidence") preserves detailed milestone evidence, while the overview and feature copy state the material scope and limits without requiring readers to find that note.')
sub('Capabilities (available):','Implementation snapshot (recorded 2026-10-14; verify current availability before advertising these capabilities):')
sub('Protection levels are OS-neutral and there are exactly two, each a set of guarantees a host proves with evidence:', 'The target protection contract is OS-neutral, with two sets of guarantees to be established by evidence:')
sub('A machine that meets part of the contract (a test-signed trusted layer, a key or traffic path on the host side, a launcher-signed report, host-readable memory) is implementation evidence, never a weaker sellable level, and is not presented as available; the attached consumer node is exactly that.', 'A machine that meets part of the contract (a test-signed trusted layer, a key or traffic path on the host side, a launcher-signed report, host-readable memory) is evidence of a partial implementation, not proof of the full contract. If a deployment is offered with limited guarantees, those limits must be explicit before purchase and must agree with the enforced admission policy. An admission rule is not a substitute for demonstrating the guarantees it is meant to require.')
sub('hardware-enforced per-app CPU isolation that keeps the ordinary host OS out of each app\'s domain', 'per-app CPU isolation intended to exclude the ordinary host OS from each app\'s domain, with demonstrated guarantees stated per backend')
sub('On a machine without confidential-computing hardware both components together are Shield protection.', 'The complete Shield design combines both components on a machine without confidential-computing hardware; neither the name nor one working component establishes the other component\'s guarantees.')
sub('That rule is stated as product behavior in market copy.', 'Market copy may state that rule as enforced behavior only for the implementations whose admission checks and evidence establish it; describe the rest as the intended policy.')
sub('in present-tense product language, with deployment facts in the evidence contexts above.', 'with component-specific availability, evidence limits and trusted parties stated alongside the claim.')
sub('**Market copy never says "VBS-like" and never names vendor or platform mechanisms** (VBS, Hyper-V, OpenHCL, paravisor, or an operating system as a mechanism): Shield is described by its guarantees (hardware-enforced per-app CPU isolation that keeps the ordinary host OS out of app domains; masked and verified GPU execution for GPUs outside a confidential boundary; OS-independent). Vendor and platform terms appear only in clearly technical implementation and evidence documentation (the dated evidence note, repository docs, this file, the claims record).', 'Lead with the demonstrated protection rather than implementation jargon. Name vendors or platform mechanisms when necessary to explain a trust dependency, compatibility limit or unproven guarantee. Technical detail can live in supporting documentation, but material security limitations must remain visible in public copy.')
sub('- Marketing copy never names hardware or TEE tech (no "H200", no "SEV-SNP" in marketing surfaces; specifics live in docs/verification and on the architecture page, where they are proof).', '- Use plain language first; include hardware and vendor details when they affect availability, protection or whom the customer must trust.')
sub('- No readiness labels or status badges in public copy; present and future are told apart by tense. Internal milestone names (M1 to M4b) appear only in the dated engineering note and repository docs, never in the hero, the cards, or a level name.', '- Public copy distinguishes demonstrated availability from experiments and future plans in ordinary language. Badges are optional, and tense alone is insufficient. Internal milestone names (M1 to M4b) belong in supporting evidence, not customer-facing protection level names.')
sub('1. The privacy outcome leads; the mechanism supports. Say "cannot see your data" before any word of attestation vocabulary.', '1. Explain the privacy outcome together with its scope and trusted parties. Never let a slogan imply a stronger guarantee than the deployment evidence supports.')
sub('2. Never ask for trust the user could verify: every claim should sit one click from its live proof.', '2. Put material limitations beside each claim, with a link to dated, reproducible evidence. Do not describe a historical result as live proof.')
sub('6. Protection is a contract a host proves, never a name for an operating system: "cannot see your data" is said only where hardware enforces it and a verified report says so, and a machine that proves part of the contract is not listed at all. Enclave Shield is the name for how that contract is met without confidential hardware; its two components are always explained separately.', '6. Protection is a property established by evidence, not a brand or operating-system name. A report proves only what its trust chain, measurement and verified fields establish. A partial implementation must not be sold as satisfying the full contract. Explain Enclave Shield\'s CPU and GPU components separately, including incomplete validation and the absence of external review.')
